SSF 200 divides intruder alarms into alarm classes depending on **resistance** against manipulation and *saboteurs* with different levels of knowledge.

## Alarm classes

- Alarm class 1 for low risk and simple installations
- Alarm class 2 for normal risk
- Alarm class 3 for higher risk and protected objects
- Alarm class 4 for very high risk

## Practical consequences

1. The class drives requirements on components and installation
2. Alarm transmission is set in relation to the class
3. Insurers often tie policy conditions to the alarm class

### Connection to standards

> EN 50131 is used together with SSF 200 to classify the whole alarm system.

| Class | Typical environment |
| ----- | ------------------- |
| 2 | Shop, office |
| 3 | Protected object, valuables depot |

```sh
search "SSF 200" --scope fysisk
```

